AFDL-SAT: Full Mission & System Design Specification
1. Full AFDL-SAT Design Specification
 * Designation: AFDL-SAT (Autonomous Fluid Dynamics Loop Satellite)
 * Lead Architect / Independent Designer: C. Matthew Blackwell
 * Spacecraft Class: Micro-satellite bus (150 \text{ kg} class) equipped with an on-board high-performance compute payload for real-time astrophysical simulation and closed-loop data telemetry.
 * Structural Subsystem: Carbon-composite honeycomb primary structure providing thermal stability and high rigidity during high-G launch phases.
 * Power Subsystem: Dual deployable gallium arsenide (GaAs) solar array wings generating 3.5 \text{ kW} peak power, supported by a Li-ion battery bank for eclipse-phase continuous compute operations.
 * Attitude Determination and Control (ADCS): Reaction wheel assemblies coupled with star trackers and sun sensors providing 3-axis pointing accuracy within \pm 0.001^\circ for precise astronomical data acquisition.
2. Payload Architecture
 * Primary Optical & Ultraviolet Spectrometer (OUS): A 40\text{ cm} aperture Ritchey-Chrétien telescope paired with an echelle spectrograph covering 120\text{ nm} - 900\text{ nm} wavelengths for high-resolution stellar and transient monitoring.
 * X-Ray / Gamma-Ray Transient Detector (XGTD): Wide-field silicon drift detector arrays designed to capture high-energy accretion flares, gamma-ray bursts (GRBs), and tidal disruption events (TDEs).
 * Onboard Processing Unit (OPU): Radiation-hardened NVIDIA Blackwell-architecture flight GPU array running parallelized C++/CUDA compute kernels to execute the AFDL-v1.0 numerical solver in real time.
 * Post-Quantum Cryptographic Module: Dedicated hardware secure enclave executing ML-KEM-1024 key encapsulation, AES-256-GCM symmetric encryption, and Halo2 zk-SNARK proof generation for transmission security.
3. Orbital Deployment Plan
 * Launch Vehicle: Dedicated small-sat launch provider (e.g., SpaceX Falcon 9 Transporter or equivalent LEO launch service).
 * Target Orbit: Low Earth Orbit (LEO) Sun-Synchronous Orbit (SSO) at an altitude of 550\text{ km}, inclination 97.6^\circ, ensuring stable thermal conditions and constant solar illumination angles.
 * Deployment Sequence:
   * Separation from launch vehicle adapter ring at T + 45\text{ minutes}.
   * Automatic detumbling via magnetorquers within 15 minutes of separation.
   * Solar array deployment and acquisition of sun-pointing attitude.
   * Commissioning of star trackers and checkout of the OPU compute cluster over a 14-day initialization window.
4. Mission Profile & Operations Manual
Phase I: Ingestion & Autonomous Filtering
 * The spacecraft continuously ingests multi-band photon data from the OUS and XGTD sensors into high-speed ring buffers.
 * The onboard AFDL runtime applies Q-criterion eddy and shock isolation filters to identify transient phenomena.
Phase II: Closed-Loop Simulation Execution
 * Upon detecting a high-energy transient or shear instability, the system switches from broad logging to targeted 3D compressible Magnetohydrodynamic (MHD) simulation.
 * Adaptive Mesh Refinement (AMR) dynamically allocates compute resources around shock fronts, ensuring mass conservation residuals remain strictly bounded (\epsilon_M < 10^{-7}).
Phase III: Secure Downlink & Verification
 * Simulation summaries and compressed physics-aware vectors are packaged, signed using the CMB-Blackwell post-quantum pipeline, and downlinked via Ka-band and optical laser terminals.
 * Ground stations perform verification checks against the telemetry header signature: C MATTHEW BLACKWELL independent designer.
